```

Why do you think that the designers of the C language made it this way?

### Answer

It is difficult to tell, but one reason is probably to ensure that there are no implicit type conversions between functions. Since C functions can return any type, including nonnumeric types, it would be difficult to guess the return type of a function, and so difficult to make implicit conversions between it and the actual type of the variable being assigned.

## Question 9.20

In C, there are no strings as such, but instead there are arrays of characters, terminated by a null character. This allows many string operations to be implemented by functions that take pointers to characters and so don't need to know the length of the string they are processing. In C++, strings are a separate type, and it is not always possible to write functions that use pointers. For example, consider the following two functions:

```c
// C-style
void printc(char* s)
{
    while (*s)
    {
        printf("%c", *s);
        s++;
    }
    printf("\n");
}

// C++-style
void printcpp(string s)
{
    cout << s << endl;
}
```

What do the two functions have in common, and what are their major differences?

### Answer

The two functions have a common purpose: they both print out the contents of a string to the standard output. The difference is that printc uses C-style strings and printcpp uses C++-style strings. The major differences are as follows:

*   The C-style function doesn't check for the string being NULL. The C++-style function doesn't need to because its string argument has to be a valid string.
*   The C-style function uses a while loop. The C++-style function uses an overloaded version of operator<<.
*   The C-style function uses printf and so must format the output. The C++-style function uses cout and so doesn't need to format the output.
*   The C-style function prints out one character at a time. The C++-style function can do this, but it probably won't, because it can be more efficient to print out the entire string at once.

